.TH CW-MIC-MIX 1 "2026" "ham-cwkeyer" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cw-mic-mix \- set up a PulseAudio/PipeWire microphone mix for cwkeyer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cw-mic-mix
.RI [ mic_source
.RI [ out_sink ]]
.br
.B cw-mic-mix
.B \-\-unload
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cw-mic-mix
creates a virtual microphone that contains your real microphone plus the CW
sidetone produced by
.BR cwkeyer (1).
Applications such as Discord, Mumble, or Audacity can then use the monitor of
the
.B CW_Mic_Mix
null sink as their input device.
.PP
The script creates three PulseAudio/PipeWire modules:
.BR module-null-sink " (cw_mic_mix),"
.BR module-loopback " (the real microphone into cw_mic_mix),"
and
.BR module-combine-sink " (cw_out,"
which sends the keyer tone to both the mix and your headphones).
The keyer sends its tone to
.B cw_out
by default when
.B CW_ALSA_DEVICE
is
.BR pulse .
.PP
Re-running the script is safe: modules left over from a previous run are
unloaded first. If setup fails or is interrupted, modules created by that
attempt are rolled back.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I mic_source
PulseAudio/PipeWire source name for the real microphone. Defaults to the
system default source. Use
.B "pactl list short sources"
to list source names.
.TP
.I out_sink
PulseAudio/PipeWire sink name for headphones or speakers. Defaults to the
system default sink. Use
.B "pactl list short sinks"
to list sink names.
.TP
.B \-\-unload
Remove the CW mix modules and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RATE
Sample rate for the null and combine sinks, 8000\(en192000 Hz (default
48000).
.TP
.B CHANNELS
Channel count for the null and combine sinks, 1\(en8 (default 2).
.TP
.B LOOP_LATENCY_MS
Requested loopback latency in milliseconds, 0\(en1000 (default 15).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B cw-mic-mix
Set up the mix using the current default microphone and output.
.TP
.B cw-mic-mix alsa_input.usb-mic alsa_output.pci-speakers
Use explicit source and sink names.
.TP
.B cw-mic-mix --unload
Tear the setup down again.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cwkeyer (1),
.BR pactl (1)
